WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:06.019
Respiratory therapists read ECGs every
day— during bronchoscopy, at the

2
00:00:06.019 --> 00:00:09.100
bedside of every mechanically ventilated
patient, and when a patient's

3
00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:10.760
hemodynamics suddenly deteriorate.

4
00:00:11.239 --> 00:00:14.520
And yet, formal ECG training often falls
to other disciplines.

5
00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:16.000
Not anymore.

6
00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:21.440
This lesson gives you systematic ECG
skills from equipment selection through chest pain diagnosis.

7
00:00:21.879 --> 00:00:27.120
We start with the three monitoring
systems— the three-lead oscilloscope for continuous rhythm

8
00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:30.120
monitoring, the five-lead system for
extended monitoring,

9
00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:33.919
and the 12-lead electrocardiograph for the
full diagnostic picture.

10
00:00:34.619 --> 00:00:36.619
Knowing which tool to reach for, and when,

11
00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:37.740
is clinical judgment.

12
00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:40.139
Lead placement gets thorough attention.

13
00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:44.040
A misplaced V1, or a swapped right-arm
lead for

14
00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:46.360
left-arm lead, doesn't just give you a bad
tracing.

15
00:00:46.979 --> 00:00:49.700
It can look like a cardiac emergency that
doesn't exist.

16
00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:53.319
The ECG grid translates into time and
voltage.

17
00:00:53.860 --> 00:00:56.619
Every interval, every segment, every
measurement

18
00:00:56.619 --> 00:00:58.439
depends on understanding those boxes.

19
00:00:58.439 --> 00:01:02.020
The five-step systematic analysis,
regularity,

20
00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:04.860
rate, P-to-QRS ratio, QRS

21
00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:08.139
width, and PR interval gives you a
repeatable framework for

22
00:01:08.139 --> 00:01:08.919
any rhythm strip.

23
00:01:09.519 --> 00:01:13.480
We apply it to normal sinus rhythm and to
every major arrhythmia you'll encounter.

24
00:01:14.419 --> 00:01:17.220
For chest pain, we walk through the
progression from ischemia

25
00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:20.779
to injury to infarction, inverted T waves,

26
00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:25.199
ST elevation, pathological Q waves, and

27
00:01:25.199 --> 00:01:28.599
the lead groups that tell you which
coronary artery and which wall of the

28
00:01:28.599 --> 00:01:29.279
heart are involved.

29
00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:33.720
Artifact gets its own section, because the
most dangerous ECG

30
00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:35.419
error isn't misreading a real rhythm.

31
00:01:35.980 --> 00:01:38.160
It's treating artifact as though it's
real.

32
00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:40.739
Assess the patient, then the monitor,

33
00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:41.599
always.

34
00:01:42.279 --> 00:01:43.440
Let's build these skills.

